      "paragraphCount": 29,
      "nativeAnchors": [
        {
          "index": 9,
          "before": "① The source's note points out that friction shifts the amplitude peak toward a lower frequency.\nMore precisely, in the linear model used here, with sinusoidal driving and resistance proportional to velocity, an amplitude peak, when present, occurs below the undamped natural frequency.\nWith very weak damping, the frequencies are very close, but this does not mean that resonance suddenly begins at one exact frequency.",
          "after": "To understand why the amplitude can grow so much, consider how the driving force transfers energy into the system.\nThe frequency matters, and so does when the force does positive work during the motion."
        },
        {
          "index": 18,
          "before": "Farther from the resonance region, energy transfer is usually less favorable and the steady amplitude is smaller.\nA small frequency mismatch may still lie within the resonance region and give a large amplitude, so unequal frequencies alone do not imply a small net work per cycle or a small amplitude.",
          "after": "Friction and other forms of resistance strongly affect resonance.\nIn steady oscillations, the net work done by the driving force over a cycle replaces the energy lost through the negative work of resistance."
        },
        {
          "index": 24,
          "before": "Far from the resonance region, the amplitude differences between these curves are usually smaller, especially on the high-frequency side, where the amplitude is small and relatively insensitive to these damping differences.\nAt low frequency, a noticeable quasistatic displacement may remain, so the claim that amplitude is small far from resonance is not unconditional for every force, system, and frequency.",
          "after": "Near resonance, a weakly damped system can gradually develop a large amplitude even under a small driving force.\nIt often takes a long time after driving begins to reach that steady amplitude."
        }
      ],
